  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 342 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nUpper level ridging builds back over the Great Plains this weekend. \nHowever, the ridge will not be as potent as it once was. As a \nresult, highs Saturday remain largely in the 90s across the region, \nwith triple digit heat expected in portions of the NE Permian Basin, \nthe Upper Trans Pecos, SE NM, and the Rio Grande valley. As it \nstands, Saturday looks to be our last day of widespread highs beyond \nthe century mark! Early next week, the core of the ridge begins to \ngradually shift eastward, toward the SE CONUS. Decreasing thickness \naloft allows for slightly cooler temperatures through the middle of \nthe week. Each afternoon Sunday through Wednesday looks to feature \nhighs predominantly in the 90s. Those in and near the higher terrain \ncan expect highs in the 80s. Triple digit heat becomes restricted to \nisolated areas along the Rio Grande next week. Overnight lows \nwithin the extended forecast are progged to range in the 60s to \n70s. By the end of the period, lows in the 60s start to become more \ncommonplace. The extended forecast remains dry for most of the \nregion, with the exception of the higher elevations and portions of \nSE NM. In these areas, moisture along the western periphery of the \nridge combines with upslope flow to promote isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms most afternoons. The best rain chances will tend to \nfavor locations in the vicinity of the Davis Mountains.",
